Yes, but only in these scenarios.

1. Try-finally block:

```java
try {
    // risky code
} finally {
    // cleanup code
}
```

2. Try-with-resources (automatic resource management):

```java
try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("file.txt")) {
    // use resource
} // resource automatically closed
```

Introduced in Java 7. It automatically closes resources that implement `AutoCloseable`. Resources are closed in reverse order of creation, `close()` is called even if an exception occurs, and suppressed exceptions are attached to the primary exception.

```java
try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("file.txt"));
     PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter("output.txt")) {
    // use resources
} catch (IOException e) {
    // handle exception
}
// Both resources automatically closed
```

