`@Component` is the generic stereotype and the base annotation for all Spring-managed components. Use it when the class does not fit another stereotype category, for example utility or helper classes.

`@Service` is a specialised form of `@Component` that carries the semantic meaning of the business logic layer. It adds no extra features but makes architecture layers obvious.

```java
@Component
public class EmailValidator {
    public boolean isValid(String email) {
        return email.contains("@");
    }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        // Business rules and logic
    }
}
```

PUT updates or replaces an entire resource at a specific URI, and can create the resource if it does not exist and the server supports it.

Key characteristics: it is idempotent, so multiple identical PUT requests have the same effect as one and failed requests are safe to retry. It replaces the entire resource, so missing fields in the request body are typically removed or set to null. The request body must include the full representation of the resource.

```java
@PutMapping("/api/users/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<User> updateUser(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestBody User user) {
    user.setId(id);
    User updatedUser = userService.updateUser(user);
    return ResponseEntity.ok(updatedUser);
}
```

Updating an existing resource returns 200 OK; creating a new one returns 201 Created with a Location header.

Generally you should not test private methods directly. They are implementation details that may change without affecting public behaviour, testing public methods should already cover their logic, and over-testing implementation makes tests brittle.

Test through public methods:

```java
public class Calculator {
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return performAddition(a, b);
    }

    private int performAddition(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

@Test
void testAdd() {
    Calculator calc = new Calculator();
    assertEquals(5, calc.add(2, 3)); // Tests the private method indirectly
}
```

If you must, use reflection:

```java
@Test
void testPrivateMethod() throws Exception {
    Calculator calc = new Calculator();
    Method privateMethod = Calculator.class.getDeclaredMethod("performAddition", int.class, int.class);
    privateMethod.setAccessible(true);
    int result = (int) privateMethod.invoke(calc, 2, 3);
    assertEquals(5, result);
}
```

Or make the method package-private with `@VisibleForTesting`. If a private method is complex enough to need its own tests, consider extracting it into a separate class.

It allows running the same test with different parameters supplied by a source annotation.

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@ValueSource(strings = {"hello", "world", "junit"})
void testStringLength(String input) {
    assertTrue(input.length() > 0);
}

@ParameterizedTest
@CsvSource({
    "1, 2, 3",
    "4, 5, 9",
    "10, 20, 30"
})
void testAddition(int a, int b, int expected) {
    assertEquals(expected, a + b);
}
```

Use `assertThrows()`, which also lets you assert on the exception itself.

```java
@Test
void testExceptionThrown() {
    IllegalArgumentException exception = assertThrows(
        IllegalArgumentException.class,
        () -> userService.createUser(null)
    );
    assertEquals("User cannot be null", exception.getMessage());
}
```

The older JUnit 4 approach used the annotation attribute:

```java
@Test(expected = IllegalArgumentException.class)
void testException() {
    userService.createUser(null);
}
```

